{"passages": [{"reference": "12.31", "verdict": "accept", "checks": {"complete_coverage": true, "no_added_claims": true, "names_and_negations": true, "lexical_fidelity": true, "uncertainties_disclosed": true}, "findings": []}, {"reference": "12.32", "verdict": "revise", "checks": {"complete_coverage": true, "no_added_claims": true, "names_and_negations": true, "lexical_fidelity": true, "uncertainties_disclosed": false}, "findings": [{"source_quote": "τῷ ἐν εἱρκτῇ κατὰ τὸ δυνατὸν ἐπιραινόμενον βοηθεῖν", "english_quote": "Its accusative form does not agree with τῷ ἐν εἱρκτῇ, and neither its attachment nor a contextually coherent contribution to the duty of assisting a prisoner is recoverable with confidence. The translation preserves its formal sense rather than silently replacing it, but no defensible contextual interpretation can presently be established.", "issue": "The note overlooks a defensible construction and consequently overstates the publication problem. The accusative participle can belong to the understood accusative subject of βοηθεῖν—the helper—rather than modifying the dative prisoner. On that analysis, the draft's literal passive wording, “while being sprinkled upon,” represents the supplied Greek without restoration. The intended significance of the sprinkling remains unexplained, but that uncertainty is not itself proof that no defensible English representation exists. The print is supplied as verified; no additional print verification is required merely because this expression is unusual.", "suggested_revision": "Rewrite the lexical and source-issue notes to explain the helper-subject analysis supporting the literal passive rendering. Preserve the formally possible middle alternative and explicitly state that the contextual significance of the sprinkling remains uncertain. Do not infer an object, identify the sprinkling with financial assistance, or emend the source without evidence. Replace the categorical publication block with a disclosure of this lexical and attachment uncertainty, and submit the revised passage and notes for fresh review.", "severity": "major"}]}, {"reference": "12.33", "verdict": "revise", "checks": {"complete_coverage": true, "no_added_claims": true, "names_and_negations": true, "lexical_fidelity": false, "uncertainties_disclosed": true}, "findings": [{"source_quote": "ὁμῶς τὸν τῆς φιλανθρωπίας ἐκθεμένου ἡμῖν λόγον", "english_quote": "The active genitive participle ἐκθεμένου has no explicitly stated genitive subject; Peter is readily supplied from the preceding speech, and λόγον functions as its object.", "issue": "The source-issue note misidentifies the participle's grammatical voice. ἐκθεμένου is a genitive singular aorist middle participle of ἐκτίθημι, not an active participle. Its middle form can nevertheless carry the sense “having set out” and take λόγον as its object. The contextual identification of Peter as its understood subject is defensible, and the main English does not require emendation.", "suggested_revision": "Change “active genitive participle” to “genitive singular aorist middle participle, here meaning ‘having set out.’” Retain the explanation of the understood subject and accusative object, along with the disclosure concerning printed ὁμῶς. Review the corrected notes afresh; this finding does not identify a corruption of the supplied print.", "severity": "minor"}]}]}
